---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const description =
  'Turn an existing quilt cut list and real fabric on hand into a practical shopping and cutting plan, with focused calculators for common quilting tasks.';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Plan Quilt Fabric You Have and What to Buy — QuiltClarity"
  description={description}
>
  <div class="page">
    <section class="hero home-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Practical quilting tools</p>
      <h1>Plan the Fabric for the Quilt You’re Already Making</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Bring the rectangular cut requirements from your pattern, notes, or
        spreadsheet. Add fabric you already own, then see what to use, what to
        buy, and how to cut the selected plan.
      </p>
      <p>
        The planner reconciles all pieces for each fabric together and keeps
        pattern comparison separate from your stock-aware shopping shortfall.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="/fabric-cutting-planner/"
          >Plan My Project Fabric</a
        >
        <a class="button secondary" href="/calculators/">Use a Calculator</a>
      </div>
      <p class="trust-line">
        Free · No account · Editable quilting assumptions
      </p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="tools-heading">
      <h2 id="tools-heading">Start with the job in front of you</h2>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Project fabric planner</h3>
          <p>
            Reconcile a cut list across measured remnants and any new fabric,
            then get a shopping list, named stock allocations, instructions, and
            diagrams from the selected placements.
          </p>
          <a href="/fabric-cutting-planner/">Create a fabric cutting plan</a>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Fabric, backing, and batting</h3>
          <p>
            Estimate repeated-piece yardage, compare backing panels, or check
            batting size and roll orientation.
          </p>
          <a href="/calculators/quilt-backing/"
            >Calculate quilt backing yardage</a
          >
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Eleven focused calculators</h3>
          <p>
            Calculate binding, HSTs, QSTs, Flying Geese, blocks, borders,
            sashing, or how many pieces fit real stock.
          </p>
          <a href="/calculators/">Choose a quilting calculator</a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="workflow-heading">
      <h2 id="workflow-heading">What you need and what you get</h2>
      <p>
        Start with rectangular cut or finished sizes, fabric assignments, and
        optional measured stock. QuiltClarity returns a buy-now list, exact
        material allocations, practical leftovers, warnings, and accessible
        visual and text cutting plans. Calculations run locally with no account.
      </p>
      <p>
        The bounded deterministic optimizer favors valid, practical cuts; it
        does not claim a mathematically proven global minimum. <a
          href="/how-it-works/">See how the workflow works</a
        >.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="learn-heading">
      <h2 id="learn-heading">Understand the assumptions</h2>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Usable width of fabric</h3>
          <p>
            Plan with the width left after selvages, not just the bolt label.
          </p>
          <a href="/guides/width-of-fabric/">Learn what WOF means</a>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Finished and cut sizes</h3>
          <p>See when seam allowance belongs in a piece measurement.</p>
          <a href="/guides/finished-vs-cut-size/"
            >Compare finished and cut size</a
          >
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Transparent methodology</h3>
          <p>Review the defaults, formulas, rounding, and optimizer limits.</p>
          <a href="/methodology/">Read how QuiltClarity calculates results</a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
